class:: Gendy1991
summary:: Second-order reflected breakpoint dynamics
categories:: UGens>Generators>Stochastic>GENDY
related:: Guides/Stochastica, Guides/Stochastica-GENDY, Classes/Gendy1977

description::
strong::Historical reconstruction.::

Reconstructs the two-level DSS method associated with GENDY3 (1991), crediting Iannis Xenakis and Marie-Hélène Serra. Each primary walk remembers an increment; its current position drives a secondary amplitude or duration walk. Source: Luque (2006), printed pp. 25–28, link::https://sergioluque.com/stochastics::; Peter Hoffmann, The New GENDYN Program (2000), pp. 32–34, link::https://rohandrape.net/ut/rttcc-text/Hoffmann2000a.pdf::.

For secondary interval width W and primary range ratio r, P becomes R(P + z*primaryStep*2*r*W, -r*W, r*W); X becomes R(X + P, lo, hi). Both levels reflect and reset. A primary walk can maintain one sign for many cycles, keeping the secondary position near a barrier; this is different from simply slowing a first-order walk. Integer rendering and original program bugs are not reproduced.

The oscillator remembers one amplitude and one incoming duration per point. Updates occur once per waveform cycle, in index order; a newly evolved first point connects from the actual previous cycle endpoint. Durations are fractional sample counts with carried remainder and a one-sample minimum. High settings therefore saturate the achievable frequency range. There is no antialiasing, automatic DC removal or loudness normalization. Start quietly; the examples use LeakDC and a short fade.

Construction/reset starts amplitudes at zero and durations at the midpoint of their bounds. Motion builds an audible waveform over the first cycles; zero amplitude mobility at construction stays silent. A later freeze retains the evolved waveform. Reset can deliberately interrupt continuity. Invalid selectors and numeric controls are conditioned to documented finite bounds; non-finite frequencies use 100/1000 Hz defaults.

Vanilla link::Classes/Gendy2:: also remembers amplitude/time increments. Its amplitude drive includes a Lehmer-style generator and its scaling differs; Gendy1991 exposes independent primary barriers and innovations using the same private PCG for both coordinates.

